# Aleph: discover a relational rule, then watch it overfit

Source: [Aleph repository](https://github.com/friguzzi/aleph).

A train is a collection of cars. Each car has properties. Can a learner explain
which trains are eastbound—and does the explanation survive a train it has
never seen?

In this lab you will inspect **a bottom clause**, learn a compact relational
classifier, deliberately force memorization, and then learn a recursive list
program. Both successful and misleading hypotheses come from the real Aleph
engine; we execute them on labelled diagnostic cases.

## 1. Learn a theory, not a lookup table

Aleph's basic cycle chooses an uncovered positive example, saturates it with
background facts to construct a bottom clause, searches for a useful
consistent generalization, and adds the selected clause to the theory.
It repeats until the positives have been covered.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  E["Choose uncovered positive"] --> B["Saturate: build bottom clause"]
  B --> S["Search: score generalizations"]
  S --> H["Add selected clause"]
  H --> R{"Positives left?"}
  R -->|yes| E
  R -->|no| T["Return learned theory"]
```

A bottom clause is a detailed explanation of one example under the configured
language. It contains far more literals than a good final rule usually needs.
Aleph's search does not guarantee the globally best theory: its standard
induction procedure selects clauses and removes covered positives greedily.

Set `SWI_ELIXIR_PATH` to the absolute checkout path and build SWI/NIF as in the
README. The learner source is already in `vendor/aleph/`.

```elixir
project = System.fetch_env!("SWI_ELIXIR_PATH")
Mix.install([{:swi_elixir, path: project}])
Code.require_file(Path.join(project, "examples/ilp.exs"))
backend = :port
```

