Change `backend` to `:nif` to repeat the same experiment with embedded SWI.
The shared helper owns and releases its session in `after`. Our Aleph adapter
serializes learning, rereads a task for every experiment, and removes learned
target clauses afterward. This matters because Aleph consumes its uncovered
example set and adds learned clauses to its background while learning.
Do not run other Aleph operations on these demonstration modules concurrently
in the same native VM; module state is shared across native sessions.

## 2. Read the language bias

The supplied training set has **five positive trains and five negative trains**.
The background describes individual cars, their lengths, shapes, loads, wheels,
and whether they are open or closed. These are relational facts, not a flat
feature vector prepared in Elixir.

The dataset's mode declarations control what Aleph can build:

```prolog
:- modeh(1, eastbound(+train)).
:- modeb(*, has_car(+train,-car)).
:- modeb(1, short(+car)).
:- modeb(1, closed(+car)).
```

| Notation | Role in this example |
| --- | --- |
| `modeh` | Permitted head of a learned clause |
| `modeb` | Permitted body literal |
| `+train`, `+car` | Input variables of the indicated type |
| `-car` | Output variable introduced by a literal |
| `#shape`, `#int` | Constants selected from a type |
| `1`, `*` | Recall bound, not the arity of the predicate |
| `determination/2` | Which body predicates may explain this target |

The relevant background and labels are ordinary Prolog facts. Other features
such as shape, load, and wheel count act as distractors. The four probe labels
are withheld from training.

```prolog
has_car(east1,car_12). short(car_12). closed(car_12).
has_car(east2,car_23). short(car_23). closed(car_23).
has_car(east3,car_32). short(car_32). closed(car_32).
has_car(east4,car_43). short(car_43). closed(car_43).
has_car(east5,car_52). short(car_52). closed(car_52).

has_car(west6,car_61). long(car_61). closed(car_61).
has_car(west7,car_71). short(car_71).
has_car(west8,car_81). long(car_81). closed(car_81).
has_car(west9,car_91). short(car_91).
has_car(west10,car_101). short(car_101).

:- begin_in_pos.
eastbound(east1). eastbound(east2). eastbound(east3).
eastbound(east4). eastbound(east5).
:- end_in_pos.
:- begin_in_neg.
eastbound(west6). eastbound(west7). eastbound(west8).
eastbound(west9). eastbound(west10).
:- end_in_neg.
```

## 3. Watch saturation become a rule

Before running, try to name a property that must hold of **one particular
car**, rather than of the train's cars considered separately.

```elixir
trains = ILPShowcase.learn(:aleph, :trains, 4, backend, inline_tasks) |> ILPShowcase.check!()
1 = trains["Clauses"]
IO.puts("BOTTOM CLAUSE FOR THE FIRST POSITIVE\n" <> trains["Bottom"])
IO.puts("REDUCED CLAUSE\n" <> trains["Reduced"])
IO.puts("INDUCED THEORY\n" <> trains["Program"])
ILPShowcase.scores(trains)
```

The adapter actually calls Aleph's public `sat(1)`, `bottom/1`, `reduce/1`, and
`induce/1`. The first three expose the manual's saturation/reduction workflow;
`induce/1` then performs full theory learning. The text above comes from those
returned terms. Search trace output is captured in `trains["Trace"]` so it
does not bury the results; print it when you want the full search history.

Read the final clause carefully: the same variable appears in `has_car`,
`short`, and `closed`. This shared witness is the point of relational learning.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  T["Train"] -->|has_car| C["One car C"]
  C --> S["short(C)"]
  C --> F["closed(C)"]
```

## 4. A trap that tests the meaning of a variable

These holdout structures are project-authored diagnostics, not a random test
split or an independent benchmark. Their expected labels follow the intended
short-and-closed concept. Their background facts are visible to Prolog; their
labels never enter Aleph's training examples.

| Train | Cars | Expected |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `probe_yes` | one short, closed car | eastbound |
| `probe_long` | one long, closed car | not eastbound |
| `probe_open` | one short, open car | not eastbound |
| `probe_split` | a short/open car and a long/closed car | not eastbound |

`probe_split` catches the superficially similar rule “some car is short and
some car is closed.” The correct concept requires both properties of the
**same car**.

```elixir
test_rows = Enum.filter(trains["Rows"], &(&1.split == "test"))
%{actual: false, expected: false} = Enum.find(test_rows, &(&1.goal == "eastbound(probe_split)"))
test_rows
```

## 5. Perfect training scores can hide memorization

Aleph's `clauselength` includes the head literal. A rule with `eastbound` in
its head and `has_car`, `short`, `closed` in its body therefore needs a limit
of **four**. Lower the limit to three and that rule becomes inexpressible.
Aleph can still cover the five training positives with ground facts.

```elixir
memorized = ILPShowcase.learn(:aleph, :trains, 3, backend, inline_tasks)
5 = memorized["Clauses"]
[train_score, test_score] = ILPShowcase.scores(memorized)
%{correct: 10, total: 10} = train_score
%{false_negatives: 1, false_positives: 0} = test_score
IO.puts(memorized["Program"])
%{training: train_score, holdout: test_score,
  mistakes: Enum.reject(memorized["Rows"], &(&1.expected == &1.actual))}
```

The unseen positive train is missed. The three negative diagnostics still
pass because the memorized facts say nothing about any new train. Training
accuracy alone cannot distinguish this lookup table from the general rule.

```elixir
for {limit, result} <- [{3, memorized}, {4, trains}] do
  %{max_literals: limit, clauses: result["Clauses"], scores: ILPShowcase.scores(result)}
end
```

Edit the limit in an extra copy of the learning cell and inspect the actual
program. Do not treat a larger limit as automatically better: more expressive
search spaces can be more expensive and can fit spurious patterns. These
experiments use zero allowed training noise, a 5,000-node search bound and a
15-second query budget. A timeout is an unknown outcome, not successful
learning or evidence that the concept is impossible.

## 6. From classification to a recursive program

The recursive task learns `mem(Element,List)` from 19 positive and six negative
examples. Its bias allows the target predicate itself in clause bodies, plus
list decomposition through equality. Here are representative labels:

```prolog
:- mode(*, mem(+any,+list)).
:- mode(1, ((+list) = ([-any|-list]))).
:- determination(mem/2, mem/2).
:- determination(mem/2, '='/2).
mem(1,[0,1]).
mem(3,[4,2,3]).
mem(0,[1,2]). % negative example
mem(3,[]).     % negative example
```

```elixir
membership = ILPShowcase.learn(:aleph, :membership, 4, backend, inline_tasks) |> ILPShowcase.check!()
2 = membership["Clauses"]
IO.puts(membership["Program"])
Enum.filter(membership["Rows"], &(&1.split == "test"))
```

The emitted theory has a head-match base case and a recursive tail case.
The training data uses numbers; our held-out cases use atoms, include a longer
list, and check both absence and the empty list. Each recursive call shortens
a finite list. The learned clauses are actually installed by Aleph and queried
by SWI; the notebook does not substitute Elixir's list membership function.

## 7. Make backend equality earn its keep

We first check each successful task against expected labels, then compare
Port and NIF predictions. Agreement alone could let two identical mistakes
pass. The deliberate memorization experiment is checked for its specific
failure pattern, rather than counted as a correct classifier.

```elixir
for {task, limit} <- [trains: 4, membership: 4] do
  port = ILPShowcase.learn(:aleph, task, limit, :port, inline_tasks) |> ILPShowcase.check!()
  nif = ILPShowcase.learn(:aleph, task, limit, :nif, inline_tasks) |> ILPShowcase.check!()
  true = port["Rows"] == nif["Rows"]
  true = port["Clauses"] == nif["Clauses"]
  %{task: task, clauses: port["Clauses"], scores: ILPShowcase.scores(port)}
end
```

Counts and times include saturation, reduction, induction and diagnostic
queries; they are not a pure `induce/1` benchmark.

## 8. Design the next experiment

1. Remove `closed/1` from the train determinations and see whether any compact
   alternative still fits. Test its meaning with a new train, not just its
   training score.
2. Raise `noise` and add a contradictory label. Inspect how the scoring and
   selected hypothesis change; training consistency is no longer the same goal.
3. Remove the recursive membership determination. Inspect which finite cases
   can still be explained and which longer lists break the learned theory.
4. Compare the two notebooks: Metagol restricts clauses through metarules and
   can invent predicates; this Aleph exercise restricts clauses through modes,
   determinations, saturation and search bounds. Neither bias is supplied by
   the examples alone.

